@node compactflash_device_automount_and_configuration "compactflash.device automount and configuration"
@{b}@{u}compactflash.device automount and configuration@{uu}@{ub}

Insert a CF card and its partitions mount by themselves; pull it and
they go away again. With the driver in a Kickstart ROM you can also boot
straight off the card. This guide covers both, and the @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text}
file that configures them.

The scanning and mounting itself is done by @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} , which is
required for all of it. Without that library @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} still
works, but as a plain mount-only device: you mount partitions yourself
through @{fg shine}DEVS:DOSDrivers/@{fg text} . See @{fg shine}ptable.guide@{fg text} (shipped with the
ptable.library archive) for the library internals.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Main}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Quick_start}

@endnode

@node Quick_start "Quick start"
@{b}Quick start@{ub}

Three modules are involved, and automount is opt-in by whether you
include the third:

  * @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text}: the I/O driver.
  * @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} : the partition scanner and automounter (RDB, MBR, GPT, flat
    FAT).
  * @{fg shine}compactflash.automount@{fg text} : boot and automount bringup. Include it only when you
    want autoboot or automount.

  @{b}Goal@{ub}                  @{b}Modules@{ub}                   @{b}Cold-boot autoboot@{ub}  
  --------------------  ------------------------  ------------------  
  ROM autoboot          @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} +     available           
                        @{fg shine}compactflash.automount@{fg text} +                      
                        @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} in                             
                        Kickstart                                     
  Resident/on-disk, no  @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} +     no                  
  automount             @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} (no                            
                        automount module)                             
  On-disk automount     @{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} line below     no                  

For a disk install, put @{fg shine}compactflash.automount@{fg text} and @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} in
@{fg shine}LIBS:@{fg text} and add this to your @{fg shine}user-startup@{fg text} :

@{fg highlight}
  LoadModule LIBS:compactflash.automount LIBS:ptable.library DEVS:compactflash.device L:fat95
@{fg text}

Automount is @{b}on by default@{ub} , so nothing else is needed to try it. A
config file is only for changing that; an example is shipped as
@{fg shine}ENVARC/cfd.prefs@{fg text} , copy it to @{fg shine}ENVARC:cfd.prefs@{fg text} and @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text} :

@{fg highlight}
  AUTOMOUNT 1
  FLAGS 0
  CONTROL_FAT -d-D+q
@{fg text}

That keeps automount on, fully unmounts every supported filesystem when
the card is pulled (the default, since there is no @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} key), and
passes fat95 @{fg shine}-d-D+q@{fg text} : no extra date stamps as file comments, and the
quiet option so a pulled card does not raise requesters.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link compactflash_device_automount_and_configuration}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link What_happens_when_you_insert_or_remove_a_card}

@endnode

@node What_happens_when_you_insert_or_remove_a_card "What happens when you insert or remove a card"
@{b}What happens when you insert or remove a card@{ub}

On insert:

  * The card is scanned and every partition is published to @{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} ,
    always, even with automount off. @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} lists them either way.
  * With automount on, partitions that are not mounted yet are mounted.
  * If you mounted a device-dostype DOSDriver by hand (for example @{fg shine}CFAUX@{fg text} ),
    automount pauses on each pass so that handler can claim its own partition
    first, then reuses it (shown as @{fg shine}CF0>CFAUX@{fg text} ) and mounts only the others,
    instead of starting a second handler on the same partition.

On removal, the @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} key decides per filesystem:

  * @{b}Default, no @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} key:@{ub} every partition of a supported filesystem ( @{fg shine}DOS@{fg text} / @{fg shine}FFS@{fg text}
    / @{fg shine}SFS@{fg text} / @{fg shine}PFS@{fg text} / @{fg shine}FAT@{fg text} ) is fully unmounted; anything else is kept and marked
    absent. The handler is stopped, the DOS node removed and the memory freed.
    Reinserting the card mounts it fresh.
  * @{b}@{fg shine}UNMOUNT <fs>@{fg text} :@{ub} only the listed filesystems are unmounted like that. A
    filesystem not listed is marked absent instead: its DOS node and handler stay
    in memory, and reinserting the same card reattaches it without re-initialising
    the handler. This is the native AmigaOS removable-media behaviour.
  * @{b}@{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text}@{ub} (or any name that is not recognised) keeps every handler.

A partition can also be served by your own mountlist instead of an
automount-built node: either your hand @{fg shine}DEVS:DOSDrivers@{fg text} handler claimed
the card itself ( @{fg shine}CF0>CFAUX@{fg text} ), or the automount adopted your node
because the names matched. @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} marks these rows in the fifth Flags
position: @{fg shine}s@{fg text} when the mount follows the @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} policy (the default: pull
the card and it is torn down with everything else, and the next card is
automounted under the synthesized name), @{fg shine}S@{fg text} when @{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC 0@{fg text}
protects it.

With @{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC 0@{fg text} the mount is kept on removal and marked absent
like @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} ( @{fg shine}[PT] static mount kept@{fg text} on serial), and reinserting a
card puts the same handler back in service. You mounted it, so only you
remove it. If you do remove it by hand (e.g. @{fg shine}Assign CFAUX: DISMOUNT@{fg text} ),
the partition stays claimed in @{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} , so the automount
does not take it over until a reboot or a fresh @{fg shine}Mount@{fg text} .

The other way around, a hand mount over a partition the automount (or
any other handler) already serves is refused: the new handler reports
@{fg shine}object in use@{fg text} and the existing mount stays alone on the partition.
@{fg shine}MOUNT_USED 1@{fg text} overrides that deliberately, at your own risk: reading
through the second mount mostly works, any write corrupts the volume,
and @{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} keeps showing the original mount. The policy is
stamped into the partition entries during the mount pass on insert,
which is what @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} shows and what the removal obeys, so a changed
@{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC@{fg text} applies to cards inserted afterwards, not to the very
next pull.

Under @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} , or for a filesystem left out of the list, the
partition stays visible in @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} while the card is out, shown absent.
A fully unmounted partition is removed from the resource and
re-published on the next insert.

A card that is still in use is not unmounted. A filesystem cannot give
up a volume something holds a lock on, an open Workbench window on it
being the usual case, because the volume node has to stay in the DOS
list for that lock to remain valid. It declines, and the partition is
kept and marked absent exactly as @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} would leave it, so its
handler stays in service. Close the window, or whatever else is holding
the volume, and the next removal unmounts it. A full ptable.library
build prints the refusal on serial unconditionally; @{fg shine}FLAGS 8@{fg text} adds the
driver's @{fg shine}[MW]@{fg text} / @{fg shine}[MA]@{fg text} lines around it.

These settings only take effect on the next mount:

  * @{fg shine}AUTOMOUNT@{fg text} gates the mount step, it never unmounts. Changing @{fg shine}1@{fg text} to @{fg shine}0@{fg text} leaves
    volumes that are already mounted alone. To clear them, pull the card or
    reboot.
  * @{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text} and @{fg shine}CONTROL@{fg text} are read by a handler when it starts. Under @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} ,
    or for a filesystem left out of the @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} list, the handler survives a card
    swap and keeps the values it started with. List that filesystem in @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} and
    reinsert the card to apply new ones.
  * @{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC@{fg text} and @{fg shine}MOUNT_USED@{fg text} are stamped into the partition entries during
    the mount pass on insert; a change applies to cards inserted afterwards.
    @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} always shows the stamped @{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC@{fg text} state ( @{fg shine}S@{fg text} / @{fg shine}s@{fg text} ), which is
    exactly what the next removal will do.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Quick_start}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Configuration__ENV_cfd_prefs}

@endnode

@node Configuration__ENV_cfd_prefs "Configuration: ENV:cfd.prefs"
@{b}Configuration: ENV:cfd.prefs@{ub}

The config is read fresh on every card insert @{b}and@{ub} every card removal, so
a change applies to the next card you insert or pull, with no reboot and
no rebuild. Because removal re-reads it too, a changed @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} applies
to the very next card you pull.

It is read from the first of three places that has it:

  1. @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text} - the live copy. Once the machine is up this is always the one
     used, so edit it for a change to apply now.
  2. @{fg shine}ENVARC:cfd.prefs@{fg text} - the saved copy. Write it too if you want the setting to
     survive a reboot.
  3. @{fg shine}SYS:Prefs/Env-Archive/cfd.prefs@{fg text} - the same file by its real path.

The last two matter at boot: Startup-Sequence assigns @{fg shine}ENV:@{fg text} and @{fg shine}ENVARC:@{fg text}
only after the automount agent has started, while @{fg shine}SYS:@{fg text} already exists.
That is how a card present at boot is mounted with your settings rather
than the built-in defaults.

Format:

  * One key per line, the value after whitespace. A missing key keeps its default.
  * Keys are matched case-sensitively as whole words, so @{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text} does not match
    inside @{fg shine}FLAGS_DOS@{fg text} . The search covers the whole file, not just line starts: a
    key name appearing as a whole word inside another key's value is picked up
    too, so do not use key names or the @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} filesystem names inside unquoted
    values.
  * @{fg shine};@{fg text} or @{fg shine}#@{fg text} starts a comment: the rest of that line is ignored, so @{fg shine}; FLAGS 0@{fg text} really
    is disabled.
  * The file is read up to 511 bytes.

  @{b}Key@{ub}             @{b}Value@{ub}                @{b}Default@{ub}         @{b}Meaning@{ub}               
  --------------  -------------------  --------------  --------------------  
  @{fg shine}AUTOMOUNT@{fg text}       @{fg shine}0@{fg text}/@{fg shine}1@{fg text}, @{fg shine}ON@{fg text}/@{fg shine}OFF@{fg text}, @{fg shine}YES@{fg text}/@{fg shine}NO@{fg text}  on              Mount partitions on   
                                                       insert. Only the      
                                                       first letter          
                                                       decides ( @{fg shine}N@{fg text} / @{fg shine}0@{fg text}       
                                                       off, @{fg shine}Y@{fg text} / @{fg shine}1@{fg text} on, @{fg shine}O@{fg text} +    
                                                       @{fg shine}N@{fg text} / @{fg shine}F@{fg text} ), so e.g.      
                                                       @{fg shine}NEVER@{fg text} reads as off;   
                                                       anything else is      
                                                       ignored and the       
                                                       default applies.      
  @{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text}           decimal              @{fg shine}0@{fg text}               Mount flags applied   
                                                       to every mount. See   
                                                       the bit table         
                                                       below.                
  @{fg shine}CONTROL@{fg text}         string               none            Mount CONTROL         
                                                       string applied to     
                                                       every mount. At       
                                                       most 31 characters.   
  @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text}         names                all             Filesystems to        
                                                       fully unmount on      
                                                       removal. Any subset   
                                                       of                    
                                                       @{fg shine}DOS FFS SFS PFS FAT@{fg text}   
                                                       ; a key with no       
                                                       recognized names      
                                                       (e.g. @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text}    
                                                       ) keeps every         
                                                       handler.              
  @{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC@{fg text}  @{fg shine}0@{fg text}/@{fg shine}1@{fg text}, @{fg shine}ON@{fg text}/@{fg shine}OFF@{fg text}, @{fg shine}YES@{fg text}/@{fg shine}NO@{fg text}  on              @{fg shine}0@{fg text} exempts             
                                                       hand-mounted (        
                                                       @{fg shine}DEVS:DOSDrivers@{fg text} )     
                                                       partitions from       
                                                       @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} : on          
                                                       removal they are      
                                                       kept and marked       
                                                       absent, the handler   
                                                       stays in service (    
                                                       @{fg shine}S@{fg text} in @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} ).       
                                                       Default @{fg shine}1@{fg text} : they      
                                                       are unmounted like    
                                                       everything else ( @{fg shine}s@{fg text}   
                                                       ). Takes effect on    
                                                       the next insert.      
  @{fg shine}MOUNT_USED@{fg text}      @{fg shine}0@{fg text}/@{fg shine}1@{fg text}, @{fg shine}ON@{fg text}/@{fg shine}OFF@{fg text}, @{fg shine}YES@{fg text}/@{fg shine}NO@{fg text}  off             @{fg shine}1@{fg text} lets a hand         
                                                       @{fg shine}DEVS:DOSDrivers@{fg text}       
                                                       mount claim a         
                                                       partition another     
                                                       handler already       
                                                       serves, at your own   
                                                       risk: two handlers    
                                                       cache one FAT         
                                                       volume, reading       
                                                       mostly works, any     
                                                       write corrupts it.    
                                                       Default @{fg shine}0@{fg text} : such a    
                                                       mount is refused      
                                                       and reports           
                                                       @{fg shine}object in use@{fg text} .       
                                                       Takes effect on the   
                                                       next insert.          
  @{fg shine}FLAGS_<fs>@{fg text}      decimal              global @{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text}    Per-filesystem        
                                                       override of @{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text} .   
  @{fg shine}CONTROL_<fs>@{fg text}    string               global @{fg shine}CONTROL@{fg text}  Per-filesystem        
                                                       override of @{fg shine}CONTROL@{fg text}   
                                                       .                     
  @{fg shine}DOSTYPE_FAT@{fg text}     hex                  detected        Mount MBR/GPT FAT     
                                                       partitions as this    
                                                       DosType instead of    
                                                       the detected one.     
                                                       See @{"Choosing the FAT filesystem" link choosing_the_fat_filesystem} .                
  @{fg shine}HANDLER_FAT@{fg text}     string               none            Where to load the     
                                                       FAT handler from      
                                                       when it is not        
                                                       already in            
                                                       @{fg shine}FileSystem.resource@{fg text}   
                                                       , e.g.                
                                                       @{fg shine}L:CrossDOSFileSystem@{fg text}  
                                                       . At most 23          
                                                       characters.           

@{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text} bits (see also the README "Mount Flags" section):

  @{b}Bit value@{ub}  @{b}Effect@{ub}                            
  ---------  --------------------------------  
  8          serial debug output (full build)  
  16         enforce ATA multi-sector mode     

The filesystem names are exactly these five: @{fg shine}DOS@{fg text} , @{fg shine}FFS@{fg text} , @{fg shine}SFS@{fg text} , @{fg shine}PFS@{fg text} , @{fg shine}FAT@{fg text}
. Any other name ( @{fg shine}NTFS@{fg text} , @{fg shine}EXT2@{fg text} , @{fg shine}PDS@{fg text} , ...) is not recognised and is
silently ignored. @{fg shine}PDS@{fg text} in particular is not valid: PFS3's @{fg shine}PDS\x@{fg text} DosType
is already covered by @{fg shine}PFS@{fg text} .

A per-filesystem key applies to partitions whose DosType high three
bytes match: @{fg shine}DOS@{fg text} = @{fg shine}DOS\x@{fg text} , @{fg shine}FFS@{fg text} = @{fg shine}DOS\x@{fg text} fast-filesystem, @{fg shine}PFS@{fg text} = @{fg shine}PFS\x@{fg text} (and
the @{fg shine}PDS\x@{fg text} alias), @{fg shine}SFS@{fg text} = @{fg shine}SFS\x@{fg text} , @{fg shine}FAT@{fg text} = @{fg shine}FAT\x@{fg text} . Use the overrides when you
want a setting on one filesystem only, for example serial debug on FAT
mounts alone.

A CONTROL string is only meaningful for filesystems that parse one. For
hot-plugged FAT cards, @{fg shine}CONTROL_FAT "+q"@{fg text} enables fat95's quiet option, so
no error requesters ("please reinsert", read or write windows) pop up
when a card is pulled with pending writes; errors go back to the calling
program instead. The fat95 documentation has the full option list.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link What_happens_when_you_insert_or_remove_a_card}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Choosing_the_FAT_filesystem}

@endnode

@node Choosing_the_FAT_filesystem "Choosing the FAT filesystem"
@{b}Choosing the FAT filesystem@{ub}

MBR and GPT FAT partitions are mounted for @{b}fat95@{ub} by default. Two keys
change that, and they do separate jobs:

  * @{b}@{fg shine}DOSTYPE_FAT@{fg text}@{ub} sets the DosType the node is mounted with. That is what selects
    the filesystem, because a handler is bound by DosType.
  * @{b}@{fg shine}HANDLER_FAT@{fg text}@{ub} says where to load the handler from, and is used @{b}only@{ub} if nothing
    in @{fg shine}FileSystem.resource@{fg text} already serves that DosType.

So to hand FAT cards to CrossDOS:

@{fg highlight}
  AUTOMOUNT 1
  DOSTYPE_FAT 0x4D534400
  HANDLER_FAT L:CrossDOSFileSystem
@{fg text}

@{fg shine}0x4D534400@{fg text} is @{fg shine}MSD\0@{fg text} , the CrossDOS DosType for FAT on media with no
partition table. Both keys are needed today: CrossDOSFileSystem is a
file in @{fg shine}L:@{fg text} , and on a machine with fat95 it never registers itself in
@{fg shine}FileSystem.resource@{fg text} , so without @{fg shine}HANDLER_FAT@{fg text} there is nothing to bind
and the partition stays @{fg shine}P----@{fg text} . If a FAT handler is ever resident with
its own DosType, drop the @{fg shine}HANDLER_FAT@{fg text} line and the @{fg shine}DOSTYPE_FAT@{fg text} line
alone is enough.

@{fg shine}HANDLER_FAT@{fg text} is useful on its own too. On a machine where fat95 is
neither in ROM nor already registered, this loads it from disk for the
ordinary auto-detect mount:

@{fg highlight}
  HANDLER_FAT L:fat95
@{fg text}

Things worth knowing:

  * @{b}CrossDOS handles the same FAT variants fat95 does@{ub} , so this is a real choice
    and not a restricted one: FAT32 and long filenames (VFAT) arrived in CrossDOS
    45.4 and AmigaOS 3.2 ships 45.39.
  * @{b}@{fg shine}DOSTYPE_FAT@{fg text} pins the mount to one partition's block range@{ub} , because a handler
    told which filesystem to be is not one auto-detecting its own partition. So
    unlike the default it does not follow a card swap by itself: keep the default
    @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} policy (or list @{fg shine}FAT@{fg text} ) with it, so the node is torn down and rebuilt
    for the next card. @{fg shine}HANDLER_FAT@{fg text} on its own does not change this - it only says
    where the code comes from, so auto-detect stays intact.
  * @{b}A wrong value does not silently fall back.@{ub} If nothing serves the DosType you
    named and no handler path resolves, the partition stays published but
    unmounted, listed by @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} as @{fg shine}P----@{fg text} . On a full build @{fg shine}FLAGS 8@{fg text} prints the
    parsed number as @{fg shine}dostype=4D534400@{fg text} in the worker trace, which is the quickest
    way to spot a typo. @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} shows the outcome in its @{fg shine}DosType@{fg text} column.
  * @{b}Changing either key needs a card pull@{ub} , or a reboot under @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} . Until
    the old node goes away its handler is still bound to that partition, and you
    do not want two handlers caching the same blocks.
  * This is about MBR and GPT cards only. RDB partitions always use the filesystem
    recorded on the card, and nothing here applies at cold boot, where @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text}
    cannot be read yet.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Configuration__ENV_cfd_prefs}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link When_a_partition_does_not_mount}

@endnode

@node When_a_partition_does_not_mount "When a partition does not mount"
@{b}When a partition does not mount@{ub}

Automount mounts a partition only when a handler for its filesystem is
already in the system. Unless you gave it a @{fg shine}HANDLER_FAT@{fg text} path (see @{"Choosing the FAT filesystem" link choosing_the_fat_filesystem} ), it
does not load a handler from @{fg shine}L:@{fg text} by itself, so a handler file sitting
unused in @{fg shine}L:@{fg text} does not count. A partition with no handler is still
scanned and listed by @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} , but stays @{fg shine}P----@{fg text} (present, not mounted);
it is never falsely shown as mounted. This is the same for every
filesystem: FFS, SFS, PFS, FAT and any other.

A handler becomes available in one of these ways:

  * Stored in the card's RDB, typical for native FFS, SFS and PFS partitions:
    @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} loads it straight from the RDB, at cold boot and on a DOS-time
    insert alike, so an RDB card needs nothing extra.
  * Built into the Kickstart ROM (for example pfs3aio, or fat95 in a custom ROM):
    registered by the handler's own startup at boot.
  * Made resident from disk with @{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} (for example adding @{fg shine}L:fat95@{fg text} to your
    @{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} line): becoming resident runs the handler's startup, which
    registers it.
  * Loaded on demand when you mount a matching DOSDriver by hand: mounting one
    fat95 DOSDriver loads fat95 from @{fg shine}L:@{fg text} and registers its shared FAT code, after
    which automount binds every other FAT partition to it.

FAT cards are the case that needs attention: MBR, GPT and whole-disk
(flat) FAT cards carry no handler in their partition table, so fat95
must come from the ROM, from @{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} , or from a mounted fat95
DOSDriver.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Choosing_the_FAT_filesystem}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Booting_from_a_CF_card__ROM_resident_}

@endnode

@node Booting_from_a_CF_card__ROM_resident_ "Booting from a CF card (ROM-resident)"
@{b}Booting from a CF card (ROM-resident)@{ub}

At Kickstart cold start, before DOS exists, the driver opens
@{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} , which walks the card's RDB, loads any filesystem
handlers stored in it, and registers each partition:

  @{b}RDB partition flag@{ub}    @{b}Result@{ub}                                
  --------------------  ------------------------------------  
  bootable (bit 0 set)  appears in the Early Startup boot     
                        device list                           
  bootable clear        mounted as a DOS volume, not offered  
                        as a boot device                      
  NOMOUNT (bit 1 set)   skipped: not mounted, not in the DOS  
                        list                                  

Both flags live in the RDB partition entry and are set by the
partitioning tool that created it. BootPri, stored in the partition
environment, does not decide bootability; it orders the boot list.

Only RDB partitions are registered at cold boot, because that runs
before DOS and @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text} cannot be read yet. MBR, GPT and flat
partitions on a card present at cold boot are published to
@{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} and listed as @{fg shine}P----@{fg text} ; the automount agent mounts them
once DOS is up, so @{fg shine}AUTOMOUNT@{fg text} , @{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text} and @{fg shine}CONTROL_*@{fg text} apply to them too.
Autoboot itself does not depend on @{fg shine}AUTOMOUNT@{fg text} . Removal is not special
either: pull the card and the partitions registered at cold boot follow
the same @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} policy as any other, so the default unmounts them and
@{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} keeps their handlers.

Cold-boot autoboot runs late in Kickstart's resident init, after the
internal IDE ( @{fg shine}scsi.device@{fg text} ) has started. That init is sequential, so if
the IDE stalls at boot (no drive connected, for instance) CF autoboot
never gets its turn either. The boot stub first does a single Gayle read
to check for a card; an empty slot returns immediately. With a card
present it tolerates slow cards by polling the media state for up to 5
s; a healthy card adds no measurable delay.

@{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} also registers a synthetic expansion board (Vendor ID
@{fg shine}65535 ($FFFF)@{fg text} , Product ID @{fg shine}1@{fg text} ) so the Early Startup boot menu sees the
device. It shows up in tools like @{fg shine}ShowConfig@{fg text} .

@{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} cannot give you this. It makes on-disk modules resident and
reboots, but only activates the @{i}first@{ui} romtag of each file, which is why
bringup lives in its own module: @{fg shine}compactflash.automount@{fg text} 's first romtag
is the after-DOS one, so @{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} runs it, and from then on hotplug
works as in a disk install. Cold-boot autoboot runs before DOS and needs
all three modules in a real Kickstart ROM. A scripted Capitoline-based
builder for a 1 MB AmigaOS 3.2.3 / 3.1 / 2.05 Kickstart lives in the
companion amigaos-kickstart-builder
(https://github.com/pulchart/amigaos-kickstart-builder) repo.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link When_a_partition_does_not_mount}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Checking_what_happened}

@endnode

@node Checking_what_happened "Checking what happened"
@{b}Checking what happened@{ub}

@{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} lists @{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} , which reflects the last-seen card
whether or not its partitions are mounted. The first character of the
@{fg shine}Flags@{fg text} column is the media state:

  @{b}Char@{ub}  @{b}Meaning@{ub}                                 
  ----  --------------------------------------  
  @{fg shine}P@{fg text}     present: the card in the slot has this  
        partition                               
  @{fg shine}I@{fg text}     invalid: the slot is still mounted,     
        but the card currently inserted has no  
        such partition, typically after a card  
        swap. Shows as @{fg shine}I--M-@{fg text} and returns to @{fg shine}P@{fg text}   
        when the original card comes back       
  @{fg shine}-@{fg text}     absent: no card in the drive. A kept    
        mount whose media is gone shows @{fg shine}---M-@{fg text}   

The remaining characters are @{fg shine}B@{fg text} bootable, @{fg shine}N@{fg text} nomount, @{fg shine}M@{fg text} mounted. @{fg shine}MFlg@{fg text} is
the resolved mount flags and @{fg shine}Ctrl@{fg text} the resolved CONTROL string. A mounted
volume shows as @{fg shine}scanname>dosname@{fg text} when its real DOS name differs from the
scanned name.

For a serial trace of a DOS-time mount, set @{fg shine}FLAGS 8@{fg text} (full build, 9600
baud). A @{fg shine}full@{fg text} build also prints the cold-boot part unconditionally,
whatever the flags say, with @{fg shine}[CFD] boot:@{fg text} for the driver and @{fg shine}[PT]@{fg text} for the
library:

@{fg highlight}
  [CFD] boot: open ptable.library ...
  [CFD] boot: BootScanPartitions(compactflash.device,0)
  [PT] cold boot: scanning for partitions
  [PT] RDB partition table
  [PT] + filesystem handler PFS v20.0
  [PT] - skip  SDH10 (no-mount) (MAC\0, 32 MB)
  [PT] + boot  SDH0 (PFS\3, 512 MB)
  [PT] + mount SDH1 (PFS\3, 1024 MB)
  [PT] + mount SDH2 (PFS\3, 495 MB)
  [PT] cold boot done, partitions registered: 3
@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Booting_from_a_CF_card__ROM_resident_}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Troubleshooting}

@endnode

@node Troubleshooting "Troubleshooting"
@{b}Troubleshooting@{ub}

  * Nothing automounts: check that @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text} does not say @{fg shine}AUTOMOUNT 0@{fg text} (the
    default is on), and that @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} is reachable, ROM-resident or in @{fg shine}LIBS:@{fg text}
    .
  * File-based install, card present but nothing happens: the driver is not loaded
    until something opens it. Add a @{fg shine}DEVS:DOSDrivers/CF0@{fg text} entry so it loads at boot.
  * Partitions listed in @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} but not mounted: automount is off, or their
    filesystem has no handler available.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Checking_what_happened}  @{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode
